<div id=3D"outline-container-orgd4b83d1" class=3D"outline-4">
<h4 id=3D"orgd4b83d1">Marshal integer overflow leads to out-of-heap read</h=
4>
<div class=3D"outline-text-4" id=3D"text-orgd4b83d1">
<p>
An integer overflow in the length-validation logic of OCaml's Marshal=20
deserializer allows a crafted serialized object to bypass all bounds=20
checks added by the CVE-2026-28364 fix, producing <b><b>heap out-of-bounds=
=20
reads</b></b> from <code>Marshal.from_bytes</code> / <code>Marshal.from_str=
ing</code> (and the C API=20
<code>caml_input_value_from_block</code>).
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-org1a510c9" class=3D"outline-4">
<h4 id=3D"org1a510c9">Root cause</h4>
<div class=3D"outline-text-4" id=3D"text-org1a510c9">
<p>
<code>runtime/intern.c</code> validates declared data length against the in=
put=20
buffer with unsigned 64-bit addition that can wrap:
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-c"><code><span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: i=
talic;">/* </span><span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;">caml_=
input_val_from_bytes, intern.c:1038</span><span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; fo=
nt-style: italic;"> */</span>
<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">if</span> (ofs + h.heade=
r_len + h.data_len &gt; caml_string_length(str))
   caml_failwith(<span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"input_val_from_string: ba=
d length"</span>);
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
<code>h.data_len</code> is fully attacker-controlled (8-byte field read str=
aight=20
from the stream for <code>Intext_magic_number_big</code>). With <code>data_=
len &gt;=3D 2^64 -=20
(ofs + h.header_len)</code>, the sum wraps to a small value and the check p=
asses.
</p>

<p>
The CVE-2026-28364 fix introduced:
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-c"><code><span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: i=
talic;">/* </span><span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;">inter=
n.c:1043 (added by the fix)</span><span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style=
: italic;"> */</span>
s-&gt;intern_src_end =3D s-&gt;intern_src + h.data_len;   <span style=3D"co=
lor: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;">/* </span><span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a;=
 font-style: italic;">wraps to a pointer </span>
<span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;">BEFORE the buffer</span=
><span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;"> */</span>
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
<code>intern_src_end</code> wraps to a location <b>before</b> <code>intern_=
src</code>, so every=20
<code>intern_check_read()</code> bound added by the fix (<code>len &gt; end=
 - src</code> with a=20
negative diff promoted to a huge <code>uintnat</code>) evaluates <b>false</=
b> for any=20
realistic length. The parser (<code>intern_rec</code>) then honors=20
attacker-controlled read lengths (<code>readblock</code> up to <code>Max_wo=
size</code> bytes)=20
against memory far beyond the input buffer.
</p>

<p>
The OCaml-side wrapper validation in <code>stdlib/marshal.ml</code> is bypa=
ssed by=20
the same wrap, via <code>caml_marshal_data_size</code> (intern.c:1116-1150):
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-c"><code><span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: =
bold;">return</span> Val_long((header_len - 16) + data_len);   <span style=
=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;">/* </span><span style=3D"color: #8=
f6f4a; font-style: italic;">wraps to 0 / negative</span><span style=3D"colo=
r: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;"> */</span>
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
<code>Marshal.from_bytes</code> (marshal.ml:55-62) calls <code>data_size_un=
safe</code> first,=20
gets a wrapped <code>len</code> (0 or negative), and its re-check <code>ofs=
 &gt;=20
Bytes.length buff - (header_size + len)</code> passes.
</p>

<p>
The same unchecked wrap exists in <code>caml_input_value_from_buffer</code>=
=20
(intern.c:1080), used by the public C API <code>caml_input_value_from_block=
</code>=20
and <code>caml_input_value_from_malloc</code> - these have no OCaml-side va=
lidation=20
at all.
</p>
</div>
</div>
